  "summary": "Designs bitten into folded birchbark — snowflakes, spirits, stories in a single breath.",
  "description": "Birchbark biting (mazinaabikiwebinigan) is the Anishinaabe art of folding thin birchbark and biting symmetrical designs into it with the eyeteeth: star-and-flower medallions, animal spirits, Mide-scroll echoes, geometric lattices — each a record of one person's breath and thought. Historically made by women as pattern references for beadwork and quillwork, and given as visiting gifts, biting nearly vanished before artist revivalists like Pat Kruse (and his daughter Gage Kruse) brought it into galleries, framing bites as fine art with printed outlines. Each piece is unique and unreproducible — the tooth is the stylus.",
  "culturalContext": "Birchbark biting carries Anishinaabe pattern memory: once winter's indoor art and beadwork reference, its revival (Pat Kruse's gallery work) turned the tooth-bitten medallions into fine-art currency, and each unique piece records one person's breath — an art of presence rather than production.",
